\end{example}

\begin{example}[Jury Stability Test]
Use the Jury test to determine if all roots of $D(z) = z^3 + 0.2z^2 - 0.35z + 0.05$ lie inside the unit circle.

\textbf{Solution:} Identify coefficients: $a_3 = 1$, $a_2 = 0.2$, $a_1 = -0.35$, $a_0 = 0.05$

\textbf{Necessary Conditions:}

1. $D(1) = 1 + 0.2 - 0.35 + 0.05 = 0.9 > 0$ \checkmark

2. $(-1)^3 D(-1) = -(-1 + 0.2 + 0.35 + 0.05) = -(-0.4) = 0.4 > 0$ \checkmark

3. $|a_0| = 0.05 < 1 = a_3$ \checkmark

\textbf{Jury Array Construction:}

Row 1: $0.05, \quad -0.35, \quad 0.2, \quad 1$

Row 2: $1, \quad 0.2, \quad -0.35, \quad 0.05$

Compute $b_k$ values:
\begin{align}
b_0 &= a_0 \cdot a_0 - a_3 \cdot a_3 = 0.05^2 - 1^2 = -0.9975 \\
b_1 &= a_0 \cdot a_1 - a_3 \cdot a_2 = (0.05)(-0.35) - (1)(0.2) = -0.2175 \\
b_2 &= a_0 \cdot a_2 - a_3 \cdot a_1 = (0.05)(0.2) - (1)(-0.35) = 0.36
\end{align}

Row 3: $-0.9975, \quad -0.2175, \quad 0.36$

Row 4: $0.36, \quad -0.2175, \quad -0.9975$

Check: $|b_0| = 0.9975 > |b_2| = 0.36$ \checkmark

Compute $c_k$ values:
\begin{align}
c_0 &= b_0 \cdot b_0 - b_2 \cdot b_2 = 0.9975^2 - 0.36^2 = 0.995 - 0.1296 = 0.8654 \\
c_1 &= b_0 \cdot b_1 - b_2 \cdot b_1 = b_1(b_0 - b_2) = -0.2175(-0.9975 - 0.36) = 0.295
\end{align}

Row 5: $0.8654, \quad 0.295$

Check: $|c_0| = 0.8654 > |c_1| = 0.295$ \checkmark

\textbf{Conclusion:} All conditions are satisfied. The polynomial has all roots inside the unit circle, and the system is stable.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Complete Discrete System Analysis]
A discrete-time control system has the closed-loop transfer function:
\begin{equation}
H(z) = \frac{0.2z + 0.15}{z^2 - 1.2z + 0.52}
\end{equation}
Analyze stability, find the poles, and sketch the impulse response character.

\textbf{Solution:}

\textbf{Pole Locations:}
Using the quadratic formula for $z^2 - 1.2z + 0.52 = 0$:
\begin{equation}
z = \frac{1.2 \pm \sqrt{1.44 - 2.08}}{2} = \frac{1.2 \pm \sqrt{-0.64}}{2} = \frac{1.2 \pm j0.8}{2} = 0.6 \pm j0.4
\end{equation}

\textbf{Pole Magnitude:}
\begin{equation}
|z| = \sqrt{0.6^2 + 0.4^2} = \sqrt{0.36 + 0.16} = \sqrt{0.52} = 0.721
\end{equation}

Since $|z| = 0.721 < 1$, both poles are inside the unit circle. \textbf{The system is stable.}

\textbf{Pole Angle:}
\begin{equation}
\theta = \arctan\left(\frac{0.4}{0.6}\right) = \arctan(0.667) = 33.7° = 0.588 \text{ rad}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Impulse Response Character:}

The impulse response will be of the form:
\begin{equation}
h[n] = C \cdot r^n \cos(n\theta + \phi)
\end{equation}
where $r = 0.721$ (decay rate) and $\theta = 0.588$ rad/sample (oscillation frequency).

Characteristics:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Decay:} Each sample decays by factor $r = 0.721$
    \item \textbf{Time constant:} $n_{0.37} = -1/\ln(0.721) = 3.06$ samples
    \item \textbf{Oscillation period:} $2\pi/0.588 = 10.7$ samples
    \item \textbf{Behavior:} Damped oscillation, stable convergence to zero
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
    % Z-plane
    \begin{scope}[xshift=-4cm]
        \draw[->] (-1.5,0) -- (1.5,0) node[right] {$\real$};
        \draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,1.5) node[above] {$\imag$};
        \draw[dashed] (0,0) circle (1);
        \fill[blue] (0.6,0.4) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$0.6+j0.4$};
        \fill[blue] (0.6,-0.4) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$0.6-j0.4$};
        \draw[dotted] (0,0) -- (0.6,0.4);
        \node at (0.3,0.35) {\small $r=0.72$};
    \end{scope}

    % Impulse response
    \begin{scope}[xshift=2cm]
        \draw[->] (-0.3,0) -- (5,0) node[right] {$n$};
        \draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,1.5) node[above] {$h[n]$};

        \foreach \n in {0,1,...,20} {
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\yval}{1.2*pow(0.721,\n)*cos(33.7*\n)}
            \draw[blue,thick] (0.22*\n,0) -- (0.22*\n,\yval);
            \fill[blue] (0.22*\n,\yval) circle (1.5pt);
        }
        \draw[red,dashed,domain=0:4.5,samples=50] plot (\x, {1.2*exp(-1.5*\x)});
        \draw[red,dashed,domain=0:4.5,samples=50] plot (\x, {-1.2*exp(-1.5*\x)});
    \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Z-plane poles and corresponding damped oscillatory impulse response for Example 20.6.}
\end{figure}
\end{example}

%==============================================================================
\section{Chapter Summary}
%==============================================================================

This chapter has established the z-transform as the fundamental tool for discrete-time control system analysis. The key concepts are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Historical Development:} The z-transform emerged from Hurewicz's work on sampled-data systems (1947) and was complemented by Jury's stability criterion (1958), providing a complete framework for discrete-time analysis.

    \item \textbf{Definition and Properties:} The z-transform $X(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} x[n] z^{-n}$ converts sequences to functions of a complex variable, with properties paralleling the Laplace transform.

    \item \textbf{S-Plane to Z-Plane Mapping:} The transformation $z = e^{sT}$ maps:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Left half-plane $\to$ inside unit circle (stable)
        \item Imaginary axis $\to$ unit circle (stability boundary)
        \item Right half-plane $\to$ outside unit circle (unstable)
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Stability Criterion:} A discrete-time system is stable if and only if all poles of its transfer function satisfy $|p_i| < 1$.

    \item \textbf{Jury Test:} Provides an algebraic method to determine whether all roots of a polynomial lie inside the unit circle without computing roots explicitly.

    \item \textbf{Practical Implementation:} Discrete transfer functions are implemented as difference equations, with pole location directly determining stability and response characteristics.
\end{enumerate}

The z-transform methodology is essential for modern digital control, enabling rigorous analysis and design of systems implemented on microcontrollers, DSPs, and computers.

%==============================================================================
\section{Problems}
%==============================================================================

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find the z-transform of $x[n] = (n+1)(0.5)^n u[n]$.

    \item Determine the inverse z-transform of $X(z) = \frac{z^2 + 2z}{(z-0.5)(z-0.25)}$.

    \item A continuous system has transfer function $G(s) = \frac{10}{(s+1)(s+5)}$. Convert to discrete time using ZOH with $T = 0.1$ s.

    \item Apply the Jury test to determine stability of $D(z) = z^4 - 0.5z^3 + 0.3z^2 - 0.1z + 0.02$.

    \item A discrete system has poles at $z = 0.8 \pm j0.4$. Find:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The pole magnitude and angle
        \item The equivalent continuous-time pole locations for $T = 0.05$ s
        \item The approximate settling time in samples
    \end{enumerate}

    \item Design a first-order discrete filter with time constant equivalent to $\tau = 0.2$ s for a sampling rate of 100 Hz. Verify stability.

    \item The difference equation $y[n] = 1.5y[n-1] - 0.56y[n-2] + u[n]$ represents a discrete system. Find the transfer function, pole locations, and determine stability.

    \item Using the Arduino experiment as a guide, predict and sketch the impulse response for a filter with $a_1 = -0.9$. Explain the alternating behavior.

    \item Prove that the final value theorem fails when there are poles on or outside the unit circle.

    \item A digital controller samples at 50 Hz. What is the maximum continuous-time natural frequency that can be represented without aliasing? What z-plane angle corresponds to this frequency?
\end{enumerate}

